    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e7ec0abe-fa1d-553d-a787-9a2b4e6600bb",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/edb-math/repairs/2026-10-27-topic-audit-2/published-probe/17592186087839/cd68f3e72f8d152bfe1c9ba160ae1d1dbd4e7bb93a26219e3fea36355f850f83.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{11}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5.5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{{11}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{121}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{121}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
